 are new to the gym but it also has a ton  of benefits for those a little bit more  experienced as we're going to see but  what that apparent simplicity is hiding  is actually a hip hinge movement and a  form of triple extension but in reverse  it's like a reverse  explosive vertical jump because what  you're doing is instead of extending at  the ankles and the knees and the hips  that wants to thrust yourself upwards  you're instead flexing at them in order  to slam the ball back downwards  this can train you to get down into that  lower position much more quickly and  explosively which is fantastic if you  want to increase your vertical jump  because when you jump you first perform  something called a counter movement you  drop down to a low position and then  leap up the faster and more explosively  you get into that counter movement the  higher your jump is going to be so if  you can explosively move down you're  going to be much more explosive leaping  up at the same time if you perform a lot  of snatches cleans deadlifts then you  also need to be training this reverse  movement you're training all of the  antagonist muscles that you use in those  lifting exercises involve hinging at the  hips  this is going to help prevent injury and  to strengthen you those positions  because your antagonist muscles provide  a stability role in all of those  exercises and they help to decelerate  you through the movement it's going to  prevent imbalances and it's something  that many people are simply missing in  their training just a fantastic exercise  for improving athleticism speed and  explosiveness and this is a fantastic  metabolic finisher a great example of  resistance cardio because it's so safe  there's no impact not lifting a huge  amount of weight you're safe to do this  for high repetitions explosively quickly  and to really get the heart rate pumping  and if you've just performed a workout  for your lats in particular for your  core then finishing with this is a great  way to get in that work capacity to  train your aerobic system so if you want  to get your cardio in this is a great  way to do that not only because it pairs  really nicely with resistance training  but also because it's a whole lot of fun  and that's another great thing about the  med ball slam is that it's actually  genuinely enjoyable it's really  cathartic slamming something against the  ground and if you're someone who doesn't  enjoy going for long runs if you find  that boring then this is the kind of  cardio the kind of resistance cardio  that you might actually be able to  convince yourself to do  unless you think this looks like you're  not working very hard or it's not very  heavy try and do maybe a few minutes of  this at the end of your workout go for  10 minutes and see how you feel the next  day i promise you're going to feel this  in all the working muscles so what are  those muscles well actually this is a  surprisingly big lat movement and if you  perform a lot of med ball slams like  this then the next day you're going to  feel it in the lats in particular a  little bit in the shoulders as well a  little bit in the core but really we  shouldn't be crunching so you're going  to feel it more in the hip flexors which  is another reason this translates really  nicely to athletic performance because  it's going to help you to bring those  legs up nice and quickly nice and  explosively or you can do a little bit  of a squatch to pick the ball up again  which means you get a bonus air squat in  there which of these you do is going to  depend somewhat on which type of  medicine ball you get you can get ones  that are designed to bounce and ones  that are designed not to bounce if you  only get one i recommend the bouncing  kind because they're much more  convenient for a bunch of other  movements too like if you're bouncing it  against a wall or tree so how do you  perform the medicine ball slam well i  described it as a hip hinge movement and  this should give you a bit of a clue  this isn't a movement where you're going  to be really engaging the abs by  crunching at the core you're not going  to be flexing and extending the spine  all that much although if you get a  little bit of spinal extension at the  top and a little bit of flexion at the  bottom it really isn't the end of the  world so yeah pick up your med ball  raise it above your head  slam down and as you can see i'm not  crunching my core i'm keeping my back  relatively flat  and i'm hinging  at my hips  you want to bring it up over your head  you don't need to swing your arms back  behind your head just bring it straight  up above and then you're going to slam  it down as powerfully and forcefully as  you can and this is something else to  think about really focus on generating  force if you're just going through the  motions here then you're not going to  get the full benefit one of the most  common questions i get on instagram when  i show med ball slams is what weight of  balls should you be using and this is  going to depend partly on your gold but  what i will say is go lighter than you  think mine is only six kilograms this is  a rare exercise where adding more weight  doesn't necessarily make for a better or  more difficult exercise because here the  angle of resistance is actually going in  the opposite direction so the heavier  the ball the easier time you're going to  have slamming it down it'll be harder to  get it to bounce but but it's only going  to make lifting it slightly harder which  is interesting in itself but for  slamming it down  lighter actually works just as well  because i mean gravity is on your side  here that added weight is just to add a  little bit of drag so you can really put  in some effort so it doesn't bounce away  all over the place so that you slow the  movement down slightly but you don't  need to pile on loads of weight to get a  decent workout here and if you're doing  this for high rep ranges then yeah six  kilograms is absolutely fine 10  kilograms now one of the coolest  variations i really love with a medicine  ball is the power throw and all you're  going to do here is you're going to  perform the slam but in reverse what  that means is you're going to lob it as  far as you can behind you while still  exploding and getting in that triple  extension so this time you really are  effectively training the exact same  muscles you would do for a vertical jump  this is the perfect counterbalance to  doing lots of slams and in some ways  it's better than using a bar to train  these muscles because there's no risk of  injuring yourself you can't hit yourself  i cannot think about it i don't need to  think about a triple pull you know i can  just launch it up in the air  give as much power as i can and go help  a leather  [Music]  the only downside of this exercise is  that you then have to go and retrieve  the ball which means you can't do it  easily for a large number of repetitions  but if you throw it retrieve it throw it  retrieve it you can get a good workout  in  do though try not to crush someone's dog  people do not like that  another fantastic variation you've seen  me doing a lot is the rotational slam  and here you're going to slam against  the wall or something and you're just  swinging your body and hurling the med  ball into the wall it should bounce off  you catch it and you throw it back again  once again get a little bit of hand-eye  coordination training there make sure  that your wall is able to withstand  repeatedly throwing a ball at it you  don't have form little cracks like some  people might do  if you do this against the tree you  really get that hand-eye coordination  element because you just don't know how  it's going to bounce off the tree it  looks something like this  but the idea behind this is your  training rotational strength your  training movement in the transverse  plane which i just made a whole video  about with grant should check it out if  you want to learn more about this but  it's basically a whole missing plane of  movement for many of us we simply don't  train  rotation is extremely important when  you're throwing when you're throwing a  punch when you're grappling when you're  pulling or pushing something even when  you're walking and running you need to  rotate so the fact that we often don't  rotate in the gym is frankly tragic and  when you use a rotational throw you'll  be training somewhat the serape effect  which is this combination of muscles  that make a kind of mexican serrape  pattern across your body which is kind  of like a sling on either side the  serratus the obliques and this allows  you to twist and exert maximum force  this is the secret to how baseball  players can pitch a ball at 100 miles an  hour and how martial artists are able to  punch with the same kind of force as a  car hitting you so this is how you can  exert maximum force from your body you  of course should be training it this is  once again a fantastic low impact way to  train that even at a higher rep range  i talk in my video on strength endurance  about how many endurance adaptations  occur locally in specific muscles you  increase blood flow to the muscle you  increase mitochondria in that muscle you  increase your ability to recruit motor  units in that muscle so if you want to  get more endurance at an activity it's  not just enough to run as well as doing  that activity you need to do something  resembling that activity for a high  repetition in a way that's going to test  your energy systems  so if you're a martial artist and you  want to be able to go for longer in the  ring without gassing out then performing  something like this for high high rep  ranges is a great way to train yourself  to be more endurant in those movement  patterns another similar variation is  the shot foot throw where of course  you're going to throw from the shoulder  same rotational benefits involving the  shoulder 